  "answer_text": "In American football, the jersey numbers worn by linebackers have changed significantly in recent years due to NFL rule updates. \n\nLinebackers in the NFL are currently allowed to wear any number from **0 to 59** and **90 to 99**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Classic & Traditional Numbers (50\u201359 and 90\u201399)\nFor decades, NFL rules restricted linebackers to specific ranges, making these numbers synonymous with the position:\n\n*   **50\u201359 (The Inside/Middle Linebacker Classics):** This is the most iconic range for linebackers in football history. If you think of a legendary, hard-hitting middle linebacker, they almost certainly wore a number in the 50s.\n    *   *Famous Examples:* Ray Lewis (52), Brian Urlacher (54), Mike Singletary (55), Junior Seau (55), Lawrence Taylor (56), Jack Lambert (58).\n*   **90\u201399 (The Edge Rushers & 3-4 Outside Linebackers):** In 1984, the NFL opened up the 90s to linebackers because the popularity of the 3-4 defense was exhausting the 50s range. These numbers are typically worn by outside linebackers who primarily rush the passer.\n    *   *Famous Examples:* T.J. Watt (90), Justin Houston (50/99), Joey Bosa (97).\n\n### 2. The Bridge Era (40\u201349)\nIn 2015, the NFL expanded the rules to allow linebackers to wear numbers in the **40s**. This was highly popular for hybrid, fast, off-ball linebackers.\n*   *Famous Examples:* Devin White (45), Fred Warner (48).\n\n### 3. The Modern Era (0\u201339 and Single Digits)\nIn 2021, the NFL dramatically relaxed its jersey number rules. In 2023, the league also approved the use of the number **0**. Because of this, linebackers can now wear **single digits (0\u20139), teens (10\u201319), and numbers in the 20s and 30s**. \n\nThis has become incredibly popular for modern, speedy, \"sideline-to-sideline\" linebackers who want to keep the single-digit numbers they wore in college:\n*   *Famous Examples:* Roquan Smith (0), Patrick Queen (6), Jeremiah Owusu-Koramoah (6), Micah Parsons (11).\n\n---\n\n### College Football (NCAA) Linebacker Numbers\nIn college football, jersey number rules are much more relaxed. The only strict positional numbering rule in the NCAA is that offensive linemen must wear numbers between 50 and 79. \n\nBecause of this, college linebackers can wear **any number from 0 to 99**. It is highly prestigious in college programs for defensive stars (including linebackers) to wear single digits like **1, 3, 5, 7, or 9**.",
